/-!
# Devissage of coherent modules along an irreducible closed subset

[Stacks, Tag 01YH] refines the criterion of [Stacks, Tag 01YG] to modules supported inside one
irreducible closed subset `Z₀ ⊆ X` with generic point `ξ`. A property `P` of coherent modules
which satisfies the two-out-of-three condition in short exact sequences, holds on the direct
images of quasi-coherent ideals on the integral closed subschemes *strictly* inside `Z₀`, and
holds on a single coherent module `𝒢` with support exactly `Z₀`, generic stalk killed by
`𝔪_ξ` and of length one there, holds on every coherent module supported inside `Z₀`.

The proof follows the source. Two-out-of-three on `0 → 𝒢 → 𝒢 → 0 → 0` already gives `P` on the
zero module, so the full criterion of 01YG is available. Applied to the property "`P`, or the
support is not contained in the given closed set" it shows that every coherent module supported
strictly inside `Z₀` has `P`. [Stacks, Tag 01YE] then embeds `i_*(ℐ^{⊕ r})` into `𝒢` for an
ideal `ℐ ⊆ 𝒪_{Z₀}`, isomorphically near `ξ`; the generic stalk of `𝒢` is a one-dimensional
vector space over `κ(ξ)`, which forces `r = 1`, and the cokernel is supported away from `ξ`,
so `P` holds for `i_*ℐ`. For another nonzero quasi-coherent ideal `ℐ'` the intersection
`ℐ'' = ℐ ∩ ℐ'` sits inside both with cokernels supported away from `ξ`, so `P` holds for
`i_*ℐ'` as well. Feeding this back into 01YG finishes the proof.

## Main results

* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.IrreducibleSupportDevissage.property_of_irreducible_support`:
  **[Stacks, Tag 01YH]**.

## Implementation notes

The hypothesis `dim_{κ(ξ)} 𝒢_ξ = 1` of the source is expressed as `Module.length` one of the
stalk over the local ring at `ξ`; together with annihilation by the maximal ideal this is the
same condition, since the stalk is then a `κ(ξ)`-vector space and length is its dimension. A
one-dimensional stalk is a simple module, which is what the argument identifying `r = 1`
actually uses: a nonzero map into a simple module out of a module whose stalk is generated by
the images of `r` copies is bijective only when `r = 1`.

An integral closed subscheme is presented as a closed immersion `i : Z ⟶ X` with `Z` integral,
and "`Z` strictly inside `Z₀`" as strict inclusion of `Set.range i.base` in `Z₀`.

**The local `implicit_reducible` attributes are what keeps the proofs free of transparency
options.** `X.Modules` carries two category instances — its own and the one it inherits from
`SheafOfModules X.ringCatSheaf` — and a stalk is written both as `TopCat.Presheaf.stalk` and as
the value of `TopCat.Presheaf.stalkFunctor`. The two spellings are definitionally equal but only
after unfolding `TopCat.Sheaf`, `Scheme.Modules`, its category instance, `Scheme.Modules.presheaf`
and `Scheme.Modules.pushforward`, so a `rw` or a `calc` step that mixes them fails at `implicit`
transparency. Declaring those definitions locally implicit-reducible makes the rewrites match;
the remaining comparisons are done with `congrArg` and `Eq.trans` in term mode, where the
expected type is known and defeq settles the instance paths.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 01YE](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01YE)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01YG](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01YG)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01YH](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01YH)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01YL](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01YL)
-/

